              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure 1.8 (b) Iron nails and copper sulphate solutions compared before and after the experiment"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.318,
            0.723,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why does the iron nail become brownish in colour and the blue colour of copper sulphate solution fades?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.35,
            0.657,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The following chemical reaction takes place in this Activity–",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.372,
            0.718,
            0.406
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_4.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{rcl}\\mathrm{Fe(s)} + \\mathrm{CuSO_4(aq)} \\rightarrow & \\quad \\mathrm{FeSO_4(aq)} \\quad + \\quad \\mathrm{Cu(s)} \\\\(\\mathrm{Copper} \\text {splitate}) & \\quad (\\mathrm{from} \\text {splitate})\\end{array}\\tag{1.24}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.412,
            0.723,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this reaction, iron has displaced or removed another element, copper, from copper sulphate solution. This reaction is known as displacement reaction.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.459,
            0.537,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Other examples of displacement reactions are",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.478,
            0.719,
            0.511
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r l } { \\mathrm { Z n ( s ) + C u S O _ { 4 } ( a q ) \\rightarrow Z n S O _ { 4 } ( a q ) + C u ( s ) } } \\\\ { \\mathrm { ( C o p p e r ~ s u l p h a t e ) \\quad ( Z i n c ~ s u l p h a t e ) } \\quad , } \\end{array}\\tag{1.25}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.516,
            0.719,
            0.55
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathrm{Pb(s)} + \\mathrm{CuCl_2(aq)} & \\rightarrow & \\mathrm{PbCl_2(aq)} \\quad \\pm \\quad \\mathrm{Cu(s)} \\\\(\\mathrm{Coper} \\; \\mathrm{cholrede}) & (\\mathrm{Lead} \\; \\mathrm{cholrede}) & \\quad  尐 \\end{array}\\tag{1.26}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.552,
            0.721,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Zinc and lead are more reactive elements than copper. They displace copper from its compounds.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.599,
            0.496,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1.2.4 Double Displacement Reaction"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.636,
            0.306,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 1.10"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.667,
            0.489,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take about 3 mL of sodium sulphate solution in a test tube.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.697,
            0.49,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In another test tube, take about 3 mL of barium chloride solution.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.727,
            0.415,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mix the two solutions (Fig. 1.9).",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.741,
            0.339,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.768,
            0.624,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You will observe that a white substance, which is insoluble in water, is formed. This insoluble substance formed is known as a precipitate. Any reaction that",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " produces a precipitate can be called a precipitation reaction"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.839,
            0.542,
            0.887
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{Na}_{2}\\mathrm{SO}_{4}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{aq}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}(\\mathrm{s})+2 \\mathrm{NaCl}(\\mathrm{aq}) \\\\(\\mathrm{SOdim} \\quad \\text { (Barium } \\quad \\text { (Barium } \\quad \\text { (SOdim) } \\\\\\text { subplate) } \\quad \\text { (hioride) } \\quad \\text { subplate) } \\quad \\text { (hioride) }\\end{array}"
        },
